Fix conversions between pointer types so that the result behaves like a pointer of the destination type, the way it does in Go. This applies to explicit conversions such as `P(p)` or `(*MyInt)(p)`, to the implicit conversions wherever a `*T` value is used as an assignable named pointer type such as `type P *T` or the other way around, to conversions through `unsafe.Pointer` between pointer types whose element types are the same or differ only in the names of the types they are made of, such as `*[2]int64` and `*[2]MyInt64` or struct types whose field types differ that way, and to the same operations done through reflection, such as `Value.Convert`, `NewAt` and `Set`.

A converted pointer has the destination type and is nil exactly when the source is nil, the zero `unsafe.Pointer` counting as nil. Otherwise it refers to the same variable as the source, whatever that variable is, and pointers to the same variable are equal once converted to a common type, including `unsafe.Pointer` and the addresses that reflection reports.

Values read through a pointer converted via `unsafe.Pointer` have the destination types, down to array elements and struct fields, and values written through it keep the types of the original variable.
